{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790425843,"nonce":"D/0WSrOlzhZvLDANes7HisUOXHk=","title":"OSR: an outlined resume point of an interpreted frame calls functions nothing compiled, through address 0","message":"Reproducer: crates/zyntax_builtins/tests/tables.rs, small_distinct_literal_lookups_hit, with the harness's TieredConfig left at enable_osr = true. The test compiles the built-in library as part of the program (no snapshot) and enters it through call_raw, so the entry and the library functions it calls run in the interpreter first.\n\nzb_dict_from_distinct is called with a 20-pair literal; its copy loop reaches the promotion threshold, and ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE=1 shows:\n\n    [osr] promotion requested for bead=8 (Interpreted { site: ... })\n    [osr] zb_dict_from_distinct site=...: outlined resume point\n    [osr] interpreted frame leaves at site=... -\u003e 0x...\n\nThe outlined region (fn-zb_dict_from_distinct_resume0-outlined-opt.hir) ends with calls to @zb_table_cap_for and @zb_dict_rebuild, neither compiled at that point (the trace prints '[reload] no entry pointer for ...' for both after the module compile). The frame then faults at pc 0, called from the region's code. With ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1, or with the interpreter only, the same test passes; with ZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD=1 and OSR on it still faults.\n\nzypy does not hit it on the same library code: its module body is compiled at tier 0 before it runs, so its loops take the compiled-frame path.","files":null}]}